00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:30.540
to mitigate that a little bit.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.110
JOSEPH VALENTE:
So you're probably

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.570
thinking at this point,
reading and writing

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.270
from a storage bucket on GCS
or from a database is going

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:39.610
to generate a ton of logs.

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:41.650
A lot of you would run
production systems that

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:43.960
face millions of
users, and every time

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:45.970
each one of those
users does an action,

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:49.210
it's probably going to read
from maybe one, if not more,

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.490
datastores on GCP.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.260
And so as you can
imagine, the logs

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:55.520
pile up very, very quickly.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.000
And so what you may want to
do is use the exempted user's

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.440
functionality to stop automated
accesses to that data.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.590
So this is usually things
like service accounts

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:08.110
or other accounts that
on a regular basis

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:13.390
talk to other services at
a lower level inside GCP.

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:16.180
One example is you may have
a production front end that

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:19.597
has a service account that talks
to a database on the backend,

00:10:19.597 --> 00:10:21.430
and he's regularly
pulling data out of there

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:24.476
and generating a ton of
data access audit logs.

00:10:24.476 --> 00:10:26.350
You may not be as
interested in this activity

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:29.740
because it's not a human
actor, so it's not someone

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:32.890
from inside your company who's
going and changing and deleting

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:33.400
resources.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:36.180
It's just the functioning
of your app, in general.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.100
And you may prefer to have
other analytics in place that

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:42.520
are less heavy duty that
collect the information to do

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:44.154
with this stuff.

00:10:44.154 --> 00:10:46.570
So what you can do here is you
can click this Add Exempted

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.540
User button and you can insert
the name of whether it's

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.240
a person or a service
account into this box,

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:56.710
and you can exempt them
from certain log types.

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:59.030
I'm not going to go ahead
and enable this just now,

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.570
but what I will say is
that you should be careful

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:02.950
when you use this feature.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:06.940
And the reason is that if
you add an exempted user

00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:10.079
to this box, they won't
generate audit logs.

00:11:10.079 --> 00:11:11.620
And so you need to
make sure that you

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:14.020
have appropriate security
controls in respect

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:15.580
of that user.

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:18.130
For example, you have
a service account.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.320
You know that it generates
a lot of audit logs.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:23.410
And so you say, I'll add it
to the exempted users list.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.327
You add it to the
exempted users list.

00:11:25.327 --> 00:11:27.160
But all the people who
work for your company

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.750
have access to that
service account

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.940
and can grab the credentials
for it and SSH into it

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:32.452
and do whatever they want.

00:11:32.452 --> 00:11:34.660
So now what you've done is
you've created a situation

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.240
where lots of people can
go ahead and do things

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.790
with service account
that won't be logged.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:41.470
So we have a couple
of other talks

