And you can find
out more about how

00:11:48.672 --> 00:11:50.380
we've got security
features in place that

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.246
help you secure those accounts.

00:11:52.246 --> 00:11:53.620
And we've just
released recently,

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.890
at GCP Next, a new feature
called binary authorization

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.090
that also helps around
securing service accounts,

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:03.060
but I'll let you guys look
that up in your own spare time.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:05.210
So I want to click
on this button

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:07.430
here that says Default
Autoconfig, as well.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:09.680
I want to talk a
little bit about this.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:13.610
You're probably thinking there's
a huge list of services here.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:18.290
What happens if I configure this
on Monday, and then on Tuesday,

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.780
someone goes and starts using
a bunch of new other services

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:23.180
that I didn't configure
audit logging for?

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:26.420
Do I have to just keep going
back and configuring new audit

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:27.922
logs for those services?

00:12:27.922 --> 00:12:30.380
And for that, we've created
this feature called the default

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:31.580
autoconfig.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:33.440
So you can click on
this button at the top.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.850
It's going to send
you to this page.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.540
This essentially allows you
to configure audit logging

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.590
for all the services.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:43.250
So even when a person starts
using a service you've never

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:47.660
configured before, you can just
select this default autoconfig,

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:51.350
save it, and then that
service will automatically

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:55.380
have those logs enabled
when people start using it.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.070
So you can see here, there's
a tool tip that says,

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:01.620
these logs are enabled as part
of the default autoconfig.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:05.130
So that's a useful
feature, as well.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.700
The last thing I want to touch
on, but I won't demonstrate,

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:10.580
is organization
level audit logging.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:14.270
So the configurations
for audit logging

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:18.080
obey the inheritance
of our IAM system,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:21.350
so if you create
an audit logging

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:24.620
config at an organization
level and you say, everything

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.330
in my organization,
log everything

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:31.130
for every service, that will be
inherited down by the projects.

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.347
And so you'll see a
tick here that says,

00:13:33.347 --> 00:13:35.930
these logs have been enabled as
part of the organization level

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.430
config.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:39.600
So that's available
to you as well.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:43.270
You'll need to do
that using our APIs.

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:46.430
You'll need to pull down the
IAM config for the organization

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.650
and enable it there.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:54.590
So that is how we enable
data access and admin read

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.240
audit logs on GCP.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:59.114
And now I'm going to jump
to Stackdriver Logging

00:13:59.114 --> 00:14:01.280
and show you a little bit
what we can do with those.

00:14:03.482 --> 00:14:05.190
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES:
So one other thing

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:07.040
Ash wanted to make
sure she set up

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:09.680
was exports, because
as we mentioned,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.930
your admin activity logs
are retained for 400 days,

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.614
but your other audit logs
are retained for 30 days.

00:14:15.614 --> 00:14:17.030
So if you want to
keep them or you

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:19.280
need to keep them for
compliance reasons,

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:22.550
you can export them to
GCS for long-term storage,

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:25.490
or to BigQuery, if you
want to keep track of them.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:28.400
And so Joe's going to
show us how that happens.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:29.780
JOSEPH VALENTE: Great.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:31.550
So I'm back in
Stackdriver Logging.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:34.100
And I can see now I've
selected this data access

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:35.600
type of audit logs.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.660
And I can see all the
data access operations

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:42.540
that are happening in
respect to this bucket.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:44.800
So there's a bunch
of list operations.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:46.345
Those are when
someone says fetch

00:14:46.345 --> 00:14:48.470
me a list of all the files
that are in this bucket,

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.907
you'll get an audit log
of the list operation.

00:14:50.907 --> 00:14:53.240
And I can see that there've
been different people who've

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.880
been doing that at my company.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.630
So as Marie said,
we're only going

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.950
to hold onto these
audit logs for 30 days.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.950
You're going to want to
do a whole lot of things

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:04.960
to these audit logs.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:08.260
You're going to want to
use them in other tools

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:11.062
that you have set up and other
processes inside your business.

00:15:11.062 --> 00:15:12.520
You're going to
want to export them

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.811
because you want to store
them for longer than 30 days.

00:15:14.811 --> 00:15:17.090
In some way, that's
a bit cheaper.

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:20.050
So we've got this export feature
inside Stackdriver Logging

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:22.390
that allows us to do that.

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:26.260
You can just hit this Create
Export button at the top

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:31.820
and it will export whatever
you have in this filter here.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.660
So right now, I'm exporting
all the GCS bucket logs

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.390
that are of the
data access type.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:42.810
But I'm actually going to want
to export all of my GCS bucket

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:45.320
logs, so I'll change the filter.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:51.020
And my export will now
work off this new filter.

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:54.230
So in order to do this, we need
to create what's called a sync.

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:57.370
So this sync is like
the point of integration

00:15:57.370 --> 00:15:58.780
with Stackdriver Logging.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.235
So I'm just going
to call it sync.

00:16:00.235 --> 00:16:03.050
I'm not a very creative person.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:06.590
So I'm going to
export into a service,

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:09.600
and we support three
different exports.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:12.790
The first one is BigQuery.

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:16.580
Why would you want to use
BigQuery to analyze your logs?

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:20.290
So as you can see here,
in Stackdriver Logging,

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:22.120
we've got a pretty
robust user interface

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.600
that you can use to
analyze the logs.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.890
We showed before about
how you can click through.

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:30.560
You can do searches and
filters up here at the top.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.070
Where BigQuery becomes
useful is if you

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:36.470
want to join multiple
data sources together.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:38.830
So you may have a
data source that

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:42.130
has the organizational
chart, for example,

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.050
of the people at your company.

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:47.680
Or you may have a
data source that

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:51.160
has a list of metadata
fields for each of the data

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.110
sources at your
company that specifies

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:56.620
what is high sensitivity
versus low sensitivity.

00:16:56.620 --> 00:16:59.350
Whatever that may be, you
may have these different data

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:01.600
sources and you want to
draw them all together.

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:06.010
And you want to run a query
that says, show me everything

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:09.339
that people who work in
my reliability engineering

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:13.510
team who have access to the
user data inside my company

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.599
have done in the last 30 days.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:20.359
You can run that sort of query
in BigQuery very, very easily.

00:17:20.359 --> 00:17:25.000
So that's why you might want to
export you logs into BigQuery.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:26.630
Cloud Storage.

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:30.100
So this is the second type
of export that we can set up.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:33.280
Cloud Storage is very
useful for storing logs

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:36.550
for a long period of time
for archival purposes.

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:39.310
So once we have the
sync set up, it's

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.830
going to start drawing
all the audit logs out

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:43.390
of our Stackdriver
Logging console

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:46.630
and into Cloud
Storage as a copy.

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:48.730
As Marie said, the
data access audit

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:50.830
logs are only
stored for 30 days,

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:53.830
whereas once they're in Cloud
Storage, they just sit there.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:55.640
And you just pay the
Cloud Storage rates,

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:59.420
which are very, very cheap
for storing this kind of data.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:01.835
So once you have them
in Cloud Storage,

00:18:01.835 --> 00:18:03.460
we're going to touch
a little bit later

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:06.220
on some security features you
can enable there to make sure

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:09.730
that people can't just go in
and delete the logs very easily.

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:12.250
But you can essentially
set them there

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.960
and it's one less thing
you need to worry about.

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:19.040
You'll have all of your logs
backed up to a cheap location.

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:21.760
And then if-- hopefully,
this doesn't happen--

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:23.790
but if an incident
happens down the track

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:27.080
and you discover that something
happened two months ago,

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.770
or 10 months ago,
or a year ago, you

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:32.680
can just go, hit that
Cloud Storage bucket,

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:35.170
pull up a copy of your logs,
and perform any forensics

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:36.945
that you need to.

00:18:36.945 --> 00:18:38.320
One thing that a
lot of people do

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:40.480
is they may store it in
the Cloud Storage bucket

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:44.650
and then if you need
to do advanced analysis

00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:48.160
of these logs, grab
them from Cloud Storage,

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:50.430
throw them into BigQuery,
and then do the analysis you

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:53.360
need to do there.

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:57.490
And then finally, we've
got Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:01.150
Cloud Pub/Sub is really useful
if you have a tool that you

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:04.540
might use regularly, like
a Sim tool or similar,

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:07.180
that you have on-site
and it's collecting,

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:10.270
let's say, all the logs from
your on-premise environment,

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:12.940
all the logs from your
other cloud services,

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:16.220
and the logs from GCP
that you might be using.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.180
And you just want to pipe
your audit logs directly

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:21.100
into that tool because
you don't want to have

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:22.200
to set up a new process.

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:25.990
You just want to bolt it
on your existing process.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:28.460
That's where Cloud
Pub/Sub is super useful.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:32.670
So you create a
Cloud Pub/Sub sync.

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:34.870
We grab a topic.

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:40.307
And you can subscribe to that
topic from your other tool.

00:19:40.307 --> 00:19:41.890
And every time a new
event is created,

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:43.431
it'll just pull it
into that new tool

00:19:43.431 --> 00:19:46.040
and you don't have
to worry about it.

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.910
So that's exports and they're
a fairly useful and powerful

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:52.356
feature that we support
inside Stackdriver Logging.

00:19:52.356 --> 00:19:53.980
We'll talk a little
bit more about them

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.660
later on in the deck, but I'll
just hand it back to Marie

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:01.340
now so we can talk a little
bit about some other stuff.

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:04.700
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES:
Now, as many of you know,

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.470
you're on-call.

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:09.830
And Ash is also on-call as
one of the two engineers

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:11.360
currently on the team.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:14.352
And one day a page
woke her up from a nap.

00:20:14.352 --> 00:20:16.560
The photos on the cat website
site weren't rendering,

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:19.500
and that's their main business,
so it's a pretty big problem.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:23.100
And so she went to the GCP
console to investigate.

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:26.540
Now, Ash knows that naming
is one of the hardest

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:28.760
things in software.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.820
And she solved the problem,
so all of the photos

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.130
are in a Google Cloud
Storage bucket that's

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:35.647
called Very Important Bucket.

00:20:35.647 --> 00:20:36.980
Can go back to the demo, please?

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:37.490
Thank you.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:38.510
JOSEPH VALENTE:
Highly secure name.

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.410
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES:
Highly secure.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:42.060
So if we go into
Google Cloud Storage,

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:44.240
her first step is
going to be looking

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:46.520
to see if the bucket's there.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:48.590
And it looks like it's not.

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:50.787
Now, if she didn't
know about audit logs,

00:20:50.787 --> 00:20:52.620
she wouldn't really be
able to troubleshoot.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.360
But since she does and she's now
very well-versed in audit logs,

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.890
she's going to go into Logging.

00:20:59.890 --> 00:21:03.220
And she's going to take a look
at these GCS bucket audit logs.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:06.040
And we're specifically concerned
with the admin activity

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:11.260
audit logs because that's
where a deletion would show up.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:14.500
So it looks like there was a
delete operation on our Very

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:17.170
Important Bucket by
Sherlock, who is still

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.030
learning how to use GCS.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:20.590
So he must have
accidentally deleted

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.440
the most important
bucket for the cats.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:26.320
And now, since there's no
alerting setups to their chat

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:28.350
channel or email
alerts set up, he

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:30.190
didn't realize it was a problem.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:31.434
Ash got paged.

00:21:31.434 --> 00:21:33.100
So there are a couple
of things that Ash

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:34.180
is going to want to do.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:36.790
First, she may want
to set up an alert

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:40.630
to let her know, via chat,
or via email, or even a page,

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:42.970
if this bucket is removed again.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:44.410
And second, she
might want to see

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:46.243
if there's anything
else that Sherlock might

00:21:46.243 --> 00:21:48.610
have done accidentally
because he's still

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:50.330
learning the platform.

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:53.790
So let's do that
second one first.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:55.680
And if we click
on this, we'll be

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:57.481
able to show matching entries.

00:21:57.481 --> 00:21:58.980
And you'll see that
our search field

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:04.490
has expanded from a single line
to our more advanced search.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:06.330
What we really want
to look for here

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:09.270
is we'll remove the
GCS bucket, because we

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.280
want to look at
all of the activity

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.800
that Sherlock has been doing.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:16.080
And a lot of creation
and deletion,

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:18.540
but also, this one--
set IAM policy,

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:21.640
which is a little suspicious.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:23.820
So we can go down and
see who Sherlock's

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.871
been giving access to.

00:22:26.871 --> 00:22:27.600
Let's see.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.515
He gave Cloud Functions
access, and Dataproc

00:22:31.515 --> 00:22:34.220
access-- all this access.

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:35.120
To whom?

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.970
Oh my gosh, to
sneakyspycat@gmail.com.

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:39.830
He must have been phished.

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.440
He's still learning.

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:44.780
But this is something
else that Ash might

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:47.480
want to set up an alert for.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:52.100
So if we want to set up, for
instance, an alert for deleting

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:55.520
our Very Important Bucket,
we're going to do the same Show

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:56.390
Matching Entries.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:58.520
So we're searching for delete.

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:01.900
Searching for Very
Important Bucket.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:03.650
Going to remove
Sherlock because anyone

00:23:03.650 --> 00:23:05.670
might delete this bucket.

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:07.520
And so now, we've got our logs.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.021
We have our filter set up.

00:23:09.021 --> 00:23:10.520
And what we're going
to do is create

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:13.530
a metric that will allow
us to alert on this problem

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.880
through Stackdriver Monitoring.

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:19.730
Gosh, lots of people have
been deleting this bucket.

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:21.110
Who would do that?

00:23:21.110 --> 00:23:27.365
So we will call this Very
Important Bucket Actions.

00:23:27.365 --> 00:23:28.740
We'll actually
make it a new one.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:35.940
And we'll go ahead and
create that metric.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:40.110
So now if we go over to
our Stackdriver Monitoring,

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:44.400
we can go into Alerting,
and we can create a policy

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:47.630
based on this metric
that we just defined.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:49.240
So we'll add our condition.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:53.800
We want a metric threshold,
so we'll select that.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:56.452
And because I work
on Stackdriver,

00:23:56.452 --> 00:23:57.910
I know that these
metrics are going

00:23:57.910 --> 00:23:59.500
to be called logging/user.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:01.930
So all of what are
called log-based metrics

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:04.900
in Stackdriver will show
up Stackdriver Monitoring

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:10.720
as logging/user and then
the name of the metric.

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:14.920
So we're looking for a Very
Important Bucket deletion.

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:19.870
And what we want to do is
alert if this is above zero.

00:24:19.870 --> 00:24:22.660
Now, there's no data available
for this elected timeframe

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:25.000
because I just created
this metric and no one's

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:26.200
deleted the bucket since.

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.460
So that's good.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:30.970
So I'll go ahead and save this.

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:33.460
I will add an email
notification to

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:38.410
cattechnologyofficer@gmail.com,
which shockingly was available

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:41.950
when I added it for this demo.

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:45.910
And then we'll name it, Very
Important Bucket is gone.

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:53.260
And so now, I'm not going to
go ahead and create and delete

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:57.160
the bucket, but if I did,
this is what my alert

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:00.054
would look like in Ash's email.

00:25:00.054 --> 00:25:01.720
So it'll tell me what
happened and it'll

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.976
give me a link to the Cloud
Console to go investigate.

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:13.220
So now Ash is starting to set
up a lot of different alerts

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:15.440
on various security things.

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:18.110
And what she quickly realized
is that all of these alerts

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:20.115
are really digging
into her napping time.

00:25:20.115 --> 00:25:21.740
Remember, the cat's
two favorite things

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:24.230
are napping and being
admired, and this website

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:28.340
is covering the admiration,
but napping is tricky now.

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:29.810
So what the cats
really need to do

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:31.850
is to automate their security.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:35.780
They have to start seeing
what the common problems are

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:37.700
and how they're
consistently solving them.

00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:39.860
And then writing some
automation to handle those so

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:42.560
they can stick to napping.

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:43.820
And so this is what Ash did.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:45.740
And this is a simple
example of how

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:48.590
to automate some of your
security using Cloud Functions.

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:55.850
So Joe told us a little
bit about setting up syncs.

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:58.340
And in this case,
Ash has noticed

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:01.370
that everyone's supposed
to apply a label to all

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:03.740
of their prod machines
when they spin them up,

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:06.140
but that's a really hard
thing to remember how to do.

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:07.610
So she wants to
automate it so she

00:26:07.610 --> 00:26:11.510
can stop going back and
adding them postemptively.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:16.070
So the first thing is to
go to the Logs browser.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:19.310
And she's going to
define a filter.

00:26:19.310 --> 00:26:21.380
And actually, I've
got a pre-baked cake

00:26:21.380 --> 00:26:24.530
in the oven that is an
export, so I will just

00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:27.380
show you the filter
we've already defined.

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:28.880
And what this is
going to do is it's

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:30.680
going to tell us
about when we have

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:34.660
a new VM in Compute Engine.

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:39.340
So it is an admin activity log,
that's what the top line is.

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:42.670
And we're inserting a
Compute Engine instance.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:45.100
And anything goes here.

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:48.160
And we've called this
audit log new instance,

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:51.370
and it's a Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:57.550
And so every time we create
a new VM in Cloud GCE,

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:00.340
we'll get a message
to the Pub/Sub topic

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:02.085
and we will trigger
our Cloud Function.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:09.820
So if we go over
to Cloud Functions,

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:12.950
we've got this function
called Add Label to New.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:18.520
VMs and it's looking at the
audit log new instance topic.

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:23.740
And if we take a look at it,
nothing has happened lately, .

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:26.260
But we can see the trigger
type is Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:27:26.260 --> 00:27:29.270
The topic is audit
log new instance.

00:27:29.270 --> 00:27:33.100
And you can't view the
source, but I swear,

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:37.130
this will add the label
end of prod to a new VM.

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:40.030
And so let's go take a look
at that and see if it works.

00:27:42.145 --> 00:27:44.020
JOSEPH VALENTE: We copied
the source directly

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:47.501
from Stackdriver [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:47.501 --> 00:27:49.750
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES: So
we'll create a new instance.

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:51.965
It will be called instance
seven, because again, we

00:27:51.965 --> 00:27:53.950
are very good at naming.

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.380
We'll make it super tiny.

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:58.730
And Create.

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:01.710
So if I take a look at
this instance right now,

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:06.020
then I would expect
it to have no labels.

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:07.100
If I can load it.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:10.760
Welcome to live demos--

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:13.370
there we go.

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:14.570
Oh, it already applied.

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:18.530
Anyway, if I had managed to open
it before my very quick Cloud

00:28:18.530 --> 00:28:21.590
Function applied, we would
have seen labels, none.

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:23.660
But now the Cloud
Function has kicked in.

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:25.690
It's applied this
end of prod label.

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:27.440
And the cats have
managed to automate away

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:30.560
this task that was taking
them 10 minutes of napping

00:28:30.560 --> 00:28:32.080
time a day.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:35.530
And so that's a lot
more napping, all told.

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.380
And this is just a
very simple example

00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:43.420
of a really powerful
and complex capability

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:45.190
that you have with
audit logs, which

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:49.870
is you can automate all sorts
of challenges or security

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:52.330
or non-security-related.

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:55.600
Can we go back to
the deck, please?

00:28:55.600 --> 00:29:02.118
So what else might you want
to automate using these?

00:29:02.118 --> 00:29:03.930
Oh, we're missing a slide.

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:04.895
Oh, well.

00:29:04.895 --> 00:29:06.520
So you might want to
automate something

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:11.500
like checking to see if new
users are part of your org,

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:15.106
and removing their ability to
do anything, if they're not.

00:29:15.106 --> 00:29:16.480
You might want to
automate adding

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:18.820
firewall rules to new VMs.

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:22.500
This is a super powerful
tool that you have available.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:25.870
So now that the cats have
automated their security,

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:27.460
they're able to nap.

00:29:27.460 --> 00:29:29.042
They're able to be admired.

00:29:29.042 --> 00:29:30.500
And they know their
site is secure.

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:32.094
So it's a really
happy ending, which

00:29:32.094 --> 00:29:34.010
I don't know how many
of you work in security,

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:37.850
but these are really rare
in the security space.

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:41.260
So everything is
great, and now Joe's

00:29:41.260 --> 00:29:42.610
going to tell you a little more.

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:43.860
JOSEPH VALENTE: Thanks, Marie.

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:48.250
So everyone loves napping.

00:29:48.250 --> 00:29:50.434
Everyone loves
getting admiration.

00:29:50.434 --> 00:29:51.850
In order to do
that, obviously, we

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:53.350
need to keep pairing
up our security

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:56.630
practices to free up our
time to allow us to do that.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:58.969
So today we've
covered audit logs.

00:29:58.969 --> 00:30:01.510
We've covered how important they
are as part of your security

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:03.370
strategy.

00:30:03.370 --> 00:30:06.700
One thing we have had
a few of is sometimes

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:10.000
we get a frantic call from
a customer saying, help,

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:14.721
something's happened and I don't
have data access audit logs on.

00:30:14.721 --> 00:30:16.720
Is there any way you can
recover the data access

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:20.640
audit logs that would have
been generated 60 days ago?

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.490
And unfortunately,
the answer is no.

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:25.520
So the audit logs get generated
when you turn them on.

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:28.030
And so you need to make
sure that you have these

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:29.600
on in advance.

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:33.190
It is a little bit of
work, as we saw before.

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:36.880
It takes a few minutes, but the
investment is well worth it.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:38.544
You get them set
up ahead of time.

00:30:38.544 --> 00:30:40.960
You have a process where you're
storing them and archiving

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:41.780
them.

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:44.195
And it's a really great
insurance policy in the case

00:30:44.195 --> 00:30:45.820
that something does
happen and you just

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:48.860
need that extra information.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:51.480
The other one is having
teams and processes in place.

00:30:51.480 --> 00:30:54.077
So it helps to
have great tooling.

00:30:54.077 --> 00:30:56.160
It helps to have people
who are trained to use it.

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:57.993
And if you don't already
have that in place,

00:30:57.993 --> 00:30:59.200
you should get that set up.

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:00.850
Auditing things
on a regular basis

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:03.580
and clearing through them
with an established process

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:05.982
is a lot easier than going
back later on and trying

00:31:05.982 --> 00:31:07.690
to set it all up at
once when you realize

00:31:07.690 --> 00:31:09.910
that something is amiss.

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:13.000
And finally, maturing only
once you've automated.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:16.180
So you don't want to go ahead
and start automating everything

00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:17.830
well ahead of time
because you think

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:19.370
it's going to be a problem.

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.084
See how you go first, see what
you're doing repetitively,

00:31:22.084 --> 00:31:24.000
and then invest the time
into automating that.

00:31:26.890 --> 00:31:30.610
So we also wanted to talk a
little bit about compliance

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:32.800
with audit logging, as well.

00:31:32.800 --> 00:31:36.160
Obviously, using audit logs for
security reasons is a big use

00:31:36.160 --> 00:31:38.680
case, but you may have
a compliance standard

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.270
or your own internal
policies that you

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:42.680
need to comply with, as well.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.100
So what can you do to
make compliance super easy

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:47.110
using audit logs?

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:49.720
The first one is using
aggregated exports.

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:52.410
And we'll dive into exactly
how this works later,

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:55.660
but aggregated exports allow
you to export all the audit

00:31:55.660 --> 00:31:58.660
logs for all your projects
in your organization

00:31:58.660 --> 00:31:59.561
into a bucket.

00:31:59.561 --> 00:32:01.060
And that's going
to be really useful

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:04.990
if you need to archive them
for a long period of time.

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.630
Like we said before,
GCS is very cheap.

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:09.094
Great way to archive your logs.

00:32:09.094 --> 00:32:11.260
And they've just announced
a new bucket lock feature

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:14.035
that can give you some
additional security

00:32:14.035 --> 00:32:16.630
to make sure the logs are even
more tamper-proof than they

00:32:16.630 --> 00:32:19.000
were before.

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:20.840
You should obviously
have them in a GCS

00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:23.604
bucket that has a really
strict security policy on it.

00:32:23.604 --> 00:32:25.270
You don't want to
have it so that anyone

00:32:25.270 --> 00:32:26.639
can access the bucket.

00:32:26.639 --> 00:32:28.180
But the bucket lock
feature gives you

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:30.400
additional security
features that

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:32.710
allow you to lock it
down even further.

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:37.499
You can read more about that
on the GCS documentation.

00:32:37.499 --> 00:32:39.790
So aggregated exports-- what
are these and why would we

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:41.470
want to do them?

00:32:41.470 --> 00:32:43.180
So an aggregated
export is where you

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:47.470
export all the logs for all the
projects in your organization.

00:32:47.470 --> 00:32:51.060
New projects get
automatically included.

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:53.530
There's a command here--

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:56.090
we won't run this
because if we run it,

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:58.870
it'll do an aggregated export of
every single project at Google.

00:32:58.870 --> 00:33:03.065
And that will just set
the data center on fire.

00:33:03.065 --> 00:33:05.530
But what you can
do is you can run

00:33:05.530 --> 00:33:08.950
your own version of
this aggregated export

00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:11.130
on your own GCP organization.

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:12.963
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES:
And we do have really

00:33:12.963 --> 00:33:15.620
detailed docs on aggregated
exports and how they work.

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:19.685
So if you search for aggregated
exports, Google, Stackdriver,

00:33:19.685 --> 00:33:20.560
you'll turn those up.

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:22.930
This is a really
common request we

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:24.760
get from users is,
how do I make sure

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:28.900
that new projects are covered
in my audit log exports?

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:31.540
And so we wanted to
make sure we covered it.

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:34.990
JOSEPH VALENTE: So head to
your favorite search engine.

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:37.090
I'm not going to
suggest which one,

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:40.570
but type in aggregated
exports, Stackdriver Logging,

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:43.390
and there's a full guide
on how you can set it up.

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:45.220
The query we have here
is just an example,

00:33:45.220 --> 00:33:47.680
so obviously, it's not going
to work on your project,

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:53.380
but it's just a G Cloud command
that you can use from the SDK.

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:57.370
That'll set up the Stackdriver
aggregated exports for this org

00:33:57.370 --> 00:33:59.450
and you'll get it
all into a bucket.

00:34:02.331 --> 00:34:03.830
The last thing we
wanted to touch on

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:05.930
was access transparency logs.

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:08.927
So we did touch on these
a little bit at the start.

00:34:08.927 --> 00:34:10.510
These are these logs
that you're going

00:34:10.510 --> 00:34:13.760
to get if support access
your information for whatever

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:15.830
reason.

00:34:15.830 --> 00:34:18.230
These are only available on GCP.

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:21.170
They're only available to
platinum and gold customers.

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:23.929
And they give you, basically,
visibility all the way

00:34:23.929 --> 00:34:27.739
down the stack to even things
that engineering are doing.

00:34:27.739 --> 00:34:30.230
So it just gives
you, again, plugging

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:33.590
that last frontier of
gaps that exist over

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:35.630
on the Cloud provider side.

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:37.429
Audit logs are great
for covering things

00:34:37.429 --> 00:34:40.429
that happen inside your own org,
but if someone calls up support

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:42.590
and says to support,
hey, I'd like help

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:45.020
because I can't access
what's in this bucket,

00:34:45.020 --> 00:34:48.110
this will make sure that if
support read the access control

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:52.400
list on that bucket, you'll
get an order log of that.

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:54.219
So let's just dive into this.

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:56.322
We've got an example access
transparency log here.

00:34:56.322 --> 00:34:58.280
I'm going to just zoom
in to each of these bits

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:03.300
so we can talk through
what's inside each of them.

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:05.450
So at the top, you
obviously get the type.

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:09.332
You can see, it's an
access transparency log.

00:35:09.332 --> 00:35:10.790
You get a little
bit of information

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:12.890
about the person
making the access.

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:16.700
You can see it was a
person who's usually

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:18.860
located in Canada, but
right now they're in the US

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:20.180
and they work for Google, LLC.

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:25.160
You get some information
about what happened.

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:28.150
So here, someone read the access
control list on the bucket.

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:31.346
We also have tooling that
stops support from reading

00:35:31.346 --> 00:35:32.220
what's in the bucket.

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:37.980
But even if they read something
as small as a label on a VM,

00:35:37.980 --> 00:35:40.249
or the name of a VM,
or the access control

00:35:40.249 --> 00:35:42.540
list on a bucket, you will
get a log of all that stuff.

00:35:45.322 --> 00:35:47.280
And then finally, the
reason why that happened.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:50.940
So you can see here that you
open up a support ticket, case

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:53.880
number bar123.

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:56.550
You can go ahead and
grab that support ticket,

00:35:56.550 --> 00:35:59.970
read everything that happened
in it, and think, yep,

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:02.790
this is kind of
what I would expect.

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:05.940
I was having trouble accessing
what was in the bucket

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:09.030
and support tech went and
checked the access control list

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:11.910
to see if I had
appropriate permissions.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.230
So these are useful.

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:14.160
You can grab them.

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:16.212
They work exactly the
same as all the other logs

00:36:16.212 --> 00:36:17.170
in Stackdriver Logging.

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:19.350
So all the things we've
just covered today

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:24.570
around setting up alerts,
setting up monitoring,

00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:25.680
those are all covered.

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:28.320
You can use these as part
of an automation suite

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.842
with Google Cloud Functions.

00:36:30.842 --> 00:36:33.300
Highly encourage you, if you
have platinum or gold support,

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:36.150
to get the set up and you
can start using them today.

00:36:39.250 --> 00:36:41.520
So if you want to
